# VulkanPCloud (`pc::`)

Reusable point-cloud global illumination, point-light rendering and BVH
shadowing for renderers that already use **VulkanWrapper**. PCloud is a
standalone module in `vulkan-systems/pcloud/`; it has no dependencies on
`vulkan-game`, a particular mesh format, a server, GLFW, Ozz, or an editor.

The six original lighting/presentation shaders are stored unchanged in
`pcloud/pcloud/shaders/`. The point cloud, world-space collision-box
sampling, 3D GI volume, shadow BVH and light-local acceleration, bricked
GI candidate lists, 16×16 tiled light selection, BRDF, tonemapping and
presentation are implemented in `namespace pc`.

## Integrate into an existing VulkanWrapper project

For a renderer using `c-build`, initialize these Git submodules:

```text
third-party/vulkan-wrapper
third-party/vulkan-systems
third-party/vulkan-headers
```

Add one build-only include in your existing `build.c`:

```c
#include "third-party/vulkan-systems/pcloud/integration.h"
```

Then, inside `build(C_Build* b)`, after registering your existing
`VulkanWrapper` static target and your application target:

```c
pc_build_paths paths={
    "third-party/vulkan-systems",
    "third-party/vulkan-wrapper",
    "third-party/vulkan-headers",
    "build/generated"
};
pc_build_targets pc_targets=pc_build_register(b,wrapper,paths);
pc_build_include_paths(client,paths);
c_link_target(client,pc_targets.pcloud);
if(pc_build_compile_shaders(paths,"build/shaders")!=0)exit(1);
```

This compiles the local VulkanGPU, VulkanRender, VulkanBVH, VulkanSpatial,
VulkanJobs and VulkanPCloud targets against the **same existing wrapper**,
without fetching or compiling a second VulkanWrapper. If your host has
already registered those systems targets, link PCloud to the existing
targets instead of registering duplicate target names. The compiler
requires `glslangValidator` on PATH; the shader helper creates and updates
the six SPIR-V files incrementally.

## Minimal runtime code

When `vk::device` and `vk::swapchain` are already initialized, prepare
one four-image-view G-buffer for **each swapchain image** and create pc:

```cpp
#include <pcloud/pcloud.hpp>

// One entry for each swapchain image, in this precise order:
// {albedo, encoded-normal, material, depth}
std::vector<pc::gbuffer> gbuffers=...;
pc::create(gbuffers);
pc::set_exposure(1.0f);
```

Feed the world with your own scene format, without migrating to the
`vulkan-game` ECS:

```cpp
auto world=std::make_shared<pc::world>();
world->lights.push_back({{0,3,1},6,{1,0.5f,0.2f},4});
world->collision_boxes.push_back({{-4,-1,-4},{4,0,4}});
world->triangles.push_back({{-1,0,-1},{1,0,-1},{0,0,1}});
pc::set_scene(world);
pc::synchronize_scene(); // Only once previous GPU submissions are idle.
```

For each submitted frame, after the host renders and makes its G-buffer
shader-readable:

```cpp
pc::camera_frame camera{eye,yaw,pitch,vertical_fov_radians};
pc::record(command_buffer,swapchain_image_index,camera);
// Submit the command buffer using your existing VulkanWrapper queue/fences.
// Only after a successful queue submission:
pc::updated(swapchain_image_index);
```

`pc::record()` internally records and synchronizes cloud, GI, lighting,
tonemapping and the final swapchain presentation pass. You do not need to
write these passes or their internal buffer/image barriers. The host still
manages acquire/submit/present semaphores, the geometry-pass-to-G-buffer
barrier, and GPU completion before replacing PCloud resources.

On resize, wait for the GPU, call `pc::release()`, recreate your swapchain
and G-buffers, then call `pc::create(gbuffers)` again. Call `pc::release()`
before shutting down the Vulkan device.

### G-buffer contract

The original shaders require this exact packing and depth behavior:

| Slot | Image | Packing |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 0 | Albedo | Linear RGB color |
| 1 | Normal | World-space normal encoded as `N*0.5+0.5` |
| 2 | Material | R=roughness, G=metallic, B=AO |
| 3 | Depth | Original engine's sampled depth mapping; background depth=1, viewmodel values below 0.1 |

The geometry pass must transition its output attachments for shader
sampling before PCloud starts. Other G-buffer layouts, alternative
projection/depth conventions, or different material representations
require an explicit adapter or shader variant, not a new PCloud engine.

### Advanced integration

If your renderer already owns a render graph, use the individual API
instead of `pc::record()`:

```cpp
pc::configuration shaders=pc::configuration::from_directory("build/shaders");
pc::presentation_target surface{images,format,extent};
pc::create(shaders,gbuffers,surface);
pc::set_scene(world);
pc::synchronize_scene();
// In the host graph: pc::cloud, pc::gi, pc::lighting,
// pc::tonemap, pc::present, with explicit resource accesses.
// pc::images(image) returns cloud point/grid/HDR/LDR resource handles.
```

`pc::present` also takes an optional overlay callback for UI rasterization
after tonemapping. All low-level API calls remain available; the simplified
API does not require you to replace your existing VulkanWrapper renderer.

PCloud currently maintains one active context and preserves the original
shader/pipeline contract. It is reusable across VulkanWrapper hosts, but
not a universal automatic Metal/D3D12 backend. Source migration and
structural checks do not establish runtime visual or performance parity;
build and GPU validation are still required on the target device.
